WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.730 --> 00:00:02.490
- [Narrator] Registered
voters in the Seattle area

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:04.990
just had the chance to
participate in what officials say

00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:08.550
is the country's most extensive
use of mobile voting yet.

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:10.940
Over 95% of the election's ballots

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:14.120
were submitted electronically,
via app or website.

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:15.417
- Supporters of mobile voting say,

00:00:15.417 --> 00:00:18.147
"If you let people cast their
ballots on their phones,

00:00:18.147 --> 00:00:19.527
"they're a lot more likely to do it

00:00:19.527 --> 00:00:22.120
"because it'll be easier
and take less time."

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:23.360
- [Narrator] But some experts warn

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:26.010
that voting on our phones
creates new privacy

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:29.550
and security problems that
we don't have solutions for.

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:32.670
Will it always be too risky
for a presidential election

00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:35.910
or is nationwide mobile
voting only a matter of time?

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:40.610
- At the end of the day, voting
software is still software.

00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:43.800
And all software is buggy.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:46.500
And there will always be
errors and security holes.

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:49.210
It's impossible to build perfect software.

00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:51.020
- [Narrator] A tech company
called Democracy Live

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:53.160
built the system that
Washington voters used

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:54.570
during the recent pilot.

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:57.260
And while Democracy Live
agrees that both software

00:00:57.260 --> 00:00:59.680
and humans can be
fallible, they're confident

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:01.240
their system is still safer

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:03.270
than some methods currently in use.

00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:05.460
- King County, which is Washington's state

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:09.540
most populous county recently
allowed mobile voting

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:13.680
for over one million voters
in a small, local election.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:16.690
Voters could use their
phones or their laptops

00:01:16.690 --> 00:01:20.270
to log onto a portal,
open up a ballot and then

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:23.580
they could choose to submit
that ballot electronically,

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:26.440
which is what we would
think of as mobile voting.

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:27.470
- [Narrator] Supporters of mobile voting

00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:30.040
say that it will make the
process more convenient

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:31.350
and boost voter turnout.

00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:33.370
In King County, election officials say

00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:35.640
that turnout this year
was double what it was

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:37.380
for this election last year.

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:39.510
Most of the roughly 6,500 ballots

00:01:39.510 --> 00:01:41.370
were submitted electronically.

00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:44.560
Only 209 were submitted
by mail or drop box.

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:47.530
- If I had to choose between
submitting a paper ballot

00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:50.640
or using the app on my phone,

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:52.810
I actually would do the paper ballot.

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:56.210
We have a lot of experience
with paper ballots over time.

00:01:56.210 --> 00:01:59.870
And so there's kind of a
big field test out there

00:01:59.870 --> 00:02:02.010
that's been going on for decades.

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:05.190
And we know that it's not easy to change

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:07.430
a large number of paper ballots.

00:02:07.430 --> 00:02:09.600
My concern about the digital environment

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:12.480
is that once those votes are collected

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:16.200
in a single database, the possibility

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:19.260
that someone could manipulate
a large number of votes

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:20.620
at once is much higher.

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:23.610
Even more importantly is
the fact that other people

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:26.320
would potentially fear
that that would happen.

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:28.000
- [Narrator] Even under
the best circumstances,

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.040
introducing new election technology

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:32.650
can add a new layer of doubt to results.

00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:35.870
- In Iowa's caucus', the
state Democratic Party

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:39.590
wanted to use an app to
collect and report results

00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:42.790
because they thought it would
make it faster and easier.

00:02:42.790 --> 00:02:45.480
Unfortunately, based on our reporting,

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:48.140
the app wasn't put through enough testing

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:50.020
and there wasn't enough planning.

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:51.880
So it ended up having glitches

00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:53.870
that have really caused problems

00:02:53.870 --> 00:02:56.770
for figuring out what
the results were in Iowa.

00:02:56.770 --> 00:02:58.840
- There was a sense that
maybe it just didn't work

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:00.670
the way it was supposed to work.

00:03:00.670 --> 00:03:03.970
And just introducing that amount of doubt

00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:07.840
into the system delegitimatizes

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:09.840
people's confidence in the vote.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:12.860
And it doesn't, I think,
give people a high level

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:15.130
of confidence that we can manage

00:03:15.130 --> 00:03:18.630
this sort of digital layer
on top of what is already

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:21.700
a pretty emotionally-fraught
voting system.

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:25.530
- Advocates are worried
after what happened in Iowa

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:27.720
that people will start to be scared

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:30.090
of using new technology for voting.

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:32.157
Supporters of mobile voting say,

00:03:32.157 --> 00:03:34.167
"They focus on security.

00:03:34.167 --> 00:03:36.927
"They plan in advance
and that they don't think

00:03:36.927 --> 00:03:40.517
"what happened with Iowa's app should have

00:03:40.517 --> 00:03:43.870
"a negative impact on
their separate efforts."

00:03:43.870 --> 00:03:45.200
- [Narrator] Running an end-to-end test

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:47.800
is crucial in most software development.

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:49.410
Creating software for smart phones

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:52.340
presents a unique challenge
when it comes to testing,

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:54.120
which is especially important

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:56.620
when creating voting technology.

00:03:56.620 --> 00:03:59.350
- We're building it so that it can run on

00:03:59.350 --> 00:04:03.190
all the thousands of possible combinations

00:04:03.190 --> 00:04:06.220
of Android phones, and IOS phones

00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:09.020
and even Microsoft phones and phones

00:04:09.020 --> 00:04:10.870
who's operating system
haven't been updated

00:04:10.870 --> 00:04:12.930
in three or four years or even more.

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:15.550
And so there's almost a limitless number

00:04:15.550 --> 00:04:17.860
of environments in which you have to prove

00:04:17.860 --> 00:04:19.760
that software can work.

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:22.310
And it's almost impossible to test it

00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:25.070
in all of those environments except,

00:04:25.070 --> 00:04:26.870
in an actual election.

00:04:26.870 --> 00:04:28.830
- [Narrator] Still, mobile
voting supporters say

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:30.140
it's important to find ways

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:33.110
to improve access and engage voters.

00:04:33.110 --> 00:04:35.460
- Some experts and some
people who advocate

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:37.960
for mobile voting say it's inevitable.

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:40.210
They say that younger generations

00:04:40.210 --> 00:04:42.470
are used to doing
everything on their phones

00:04:42.470 --> 00:04:45.080
and they want to meet
voters where they are.

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:48.060
- Maybe when every single car on the road

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:51.070
is driven by a robot and
a generation of people

00:04:51.070 --> 00:04:53.660
can't even imagine that a human being

00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:57.417
would have ever driven a
car, we'll also be saying,

00:04:57.417 --> 00:04:59.807
"God, can you imagine
that people used to vote

00:04:59.807 --> 00:05:01.430
"on pieces of paper?"

00:05:01.430 --> 00:05:04.460
I can see that coming and
I think we'll be there,

00:05:04.460 --> 00:05:06.256
but I think it's 20 years, not 10.

00:05:06.256 --> 00:05:09.589
(upbeat cheerful music)

